1
00:00:01,100 --> 00:00:07,346
Welcome to our Knowledge Graph-Driven, Constraints-Aware AI-Based Food Formulation Platform.

2
00:00:07,296 --> 00:00:19,634
This platform is designed to accelerate scientific food product development by combining artificial intelligence, knowledge graphs, ingredient intelligence, and constraint-based formulation.

3
00:00:19,634 --> 00:00:33,419
The objective is to help food scientists and product developers identify suitable ingredients, generate formulation candidates, evaluate product characteristics, and make evidence-supported formulation decisions.

4
00:00:34,600 --> 00:00:41,366
Let's begin with the home page, which serves as the primary entry point for the formulation process.

5
00:00:41,316 --> 00:00:47,519
Users can describe their desired food product using a natural-language product brief.

6
00:00:47,519 --> 00:00:58,450
This brief contains the product category, ingredient preferences, nutritional targets, functional requirements, flavor profile, and shelf-life expectations.

7
00:00:58,450 --> 00:01:04,217
For this demonstration, we will work with a vegan white chocolate formulation.

8
00:01:04,217 --> 00:01:11,186
The system takes this product description and prepares it for structured analysis and formulation.

9
00:01:11,100 --> 00:01:15,894
After entering the platform, we reach the formulation dashboard.

10
00:01:15,844 --> 00:01:29,053
The dashboard provides an overview of the formulation workflow, including candidate ingredients, formulation progress, nutritional information, functional properties, and evaluation results.

11
00:01:29,053 --> 00:01:41,192
The platform organizes formulation information into multiple dimensions, allowing users to understand not only which ingredients are selected but also why they are suitable.

12
00:01:41,192 --> 00:01:51,913
The dashboard also provides analytical summaries and visual representations of formulation performance, helping users compare different candidate formulations.

13
00:01:53,100 --> 00:01:57,250
Next, we move to the ingredient selection stage.

14
00:01:57,200 --> 00:02:07,663
The platform explores available ingredients from its underlying ingredient knowledge base and identifies candidates relevant to the product requirements.

15
00:02:07,662 --> 00:02:19,237
Each candidate is associated with structured information such as ingredient category, functional properties, nutritional composition, and suitability information.

16
00:02:19,238 --> 00:02:30,225
Rather than selecting ingredients using simple keyword matching alone, the platform uses relationships within the Knowledge Graph to support ingredient discovery.

17
00:02:30,225 --> 00:02:39,088
This makes it possible to explore ingredients based on their functional roles and relationships with other food components.

18
00:02:39,088 --> 00:02:45,925
The selected candidates are then passed to the subsequent formulation and evaluation stages.

19
00:02:48,100 --> 00:02:53,862
An important component of this platform is constraint-aware formulation.

20
00:02:53,812 --> 00:03:02,012
The system translates product requirements into structured constraints that can be used during candidate evaluation.

21
00:03:02,012 --> 00:03:09,575
These constraints are divided into two major categories: hard constraints and soft constraints.

22
00:03:09,575 --> 00:03:15,837
Hard constraints represent mandatory conditions that a formulation must satisfy.

23
00:03:15,838 --> 00:03:24,500
For example, since our target product is vegan white chocolate, dairy-derived ingredients must be excluded.

24
00:03:24,500 --> 00:03:35,050
Soft constraints represent preferred characteristics, such as a creamy texture, a particular flavor profile, or a preferred nutritional composition.

25
00:03:35,050 --> 00:03:45,588
The platform also supports numerical constraints, including minimum protein content, maximum sugar content, and minimum cocoa butter concentration.

26
00:03:45,588 --> 00:03:52,050
Each constraint can be associated with a condition, priority, and importance weight.

27
00:03:52,050 --> 00:04:01,350
This structured representation allows the formulation engine to distinguish between mandatory requirements and optimization preferences.

28
00:04:01,350 --> 00:04:10,938
The objective is not simply to generate a recipe, but to generate candidates that are consistent with the specified product requirements.

29
00:04:13,100 --> 00:04:17,938
Now we move to the functional and texture evaluation stage.

30
00:04:17,887 --> 00:04:23,900
Ingredient selection alone is not sufficient for successful food formulation.

31
00:04:23,900 --> 00:04:31,612
Ingredients must also contribute to the required physical and functional characteristics of the final product.

32
00:04:31,613 --> 00:04:39,825
For this reason, the platform evaluates candidate sets against different functional and textural requirements.

33
00:04:39,825 --> 00:04:50,988
For our vegan white chocolate example, important characteristics include fat-system stability, smoothness, creamy mouthfeel, and melting behaviour.

34
00:04:50,988 --> 00:04:57,887
The system presents the relevant functional attributes and their associated evaluation results.

35
00:04:57,887 --> 00:05:07,000
This stage helps identify whether the selected combination of ingredients is suitable for the intended product characteristics.

36
00:05:07,000 --> 00:05:15,938
It also provides a basis for comparing different ingredient combinations rather than relying entirely on manual selection.

37
00:05:15,938 --> 00:05:28,938
These evaluations are intended as computational screening and decision-support results; experimental laboratory validation remains necessary to confirm actual product performance.

38
00:05:31,100 --> 00:05:39,550
After the evaluation process, the platform organizes the available ingredient combinations into candidate sets.

39
00:05:39,500 --> 00:05:44,350
Each candidate set represents a possible formulation direction.

40
00:05:44,350 --> 00:05:55,125
Users can compare these candidates using their ingredient composition, functional suitability, nutritional characteristics, and constraint compliance.

41
00:05:55,125 --> 00:06:03,413
The system provides a structured view of the candidate results, making it easier to identify promising combinations.

42
00:06:03,413 --> 00:06:12,525
This approach supports systematic comparison and reduces the need to manually inspect every possible ingredient combination.

43
00:06:12,525 --> 00:06:21,962
The objective is to shortlist formulations that offer a reasonable balance between product requirements and ingredient functionality.

44
00:06:24,100 --> 00:06:29,500
Here, we return to the formulation results and analytical dashboard.

45
00:06:29,450 --> 00:06:35,600
The dashboard brings together the information generated throughout the formulation workflow.

46
00:06:35,600 --> 00:06:45,713
Users can examine the selected ingredients, candidate formulation scores, nutritional information, and different evaluation parameters.

47
00:06:45,712 --> 00:06:51,962
The visual summaries help communicate the strengths and limitations of each candidate.

48
00:06:51,962 --> 00:07:01,812
Instead of presenting only a final recipe, the platform provides supporting information that helps explain the formulation decision.

49
00:07:01,812 --> 00:07:11,375
This is particularly important in research-oriented food product development, where transparency and traceability are essential.

50
00:07:15,100 --> 00:07:18,888
Next, we explore the Ingredient Library.

51
00:07:18,837 --> 00:07:27,362
The Ingredient Library acts as a centralized repository of food ingredients and their associated information.

52
00:07:27,363 --> 00:07:37,825
Each ingredient can contain details such as its name, category, functional role, nutritional composition, and other available properties.

53
00:07:37,825 --> 00:07:49,825
This structured information is important because the quality of formulation decisions depends heavily on the quality and organization of the underlying ingredient data.

54
00:07:49,825 --> 00:07:58,500
The library also provides an interface for exploring individual ingredients and their associated information.

55
00:07:58,500 --> 00:08:10,775
By maintaining ingredient information in a standardized structure, the platform creates a reusable foundation for multiple food product categories and formulation tasks.

56
00:08:30,100 --> 00:08:37,712
Now, let's explore one of the most important components of our platform: the Food Knowledge Graph.

57
00:08:37,663 --> 00:08:44,562
The Knowledge Graph represents food-related information through interconnected nodes and relationships.

58
00:08:44,562 --> 00:08:55,250
Unlike a conventional database that primarily stores information in tables, a Knowledge Graph captures semantic relationships between different entities.

59
00:08:55,250 --> 00:09:07,075
For example, an ingredient can be connected to its food category, functional role, nutritional properties, flavor characteristics, and other related entities.

60
00:09:07,075 --> 00:09:16,513
These connections allow the platform to retrieve information through relationships rather than depending only on exact textual matches.

61
00:09:16,513 --> 00:09:21,988
The graph explorer provides a visual representation of these connections.

62
00:09:21,987 --> 00:09:32,037
Users can inspect individual nodes, explore their neighbouring entities, and understand how different food concepts are interconnected.

63
00:09:32,038 --> 00:09:41,275
The platform also supports different levels of graph exploration, from individual ingredient relationships to broader subgraphs.

64
00:09:41,275 --> 00:09:54,025
This graph-based representation is particularly useful for identifying related ingredients, understanding functional associations, and supporting context-aware ingredient retrieval.

65
00:09:54,025 --> 00:10:00,612
The Knowledge Graph therefore acts as the structured knowledge foundation of the formulation system.

66
00:10:00,612 --> 00:10:07,425
It connects ingredient intelligence with the constraint-aware retrieval and formulation workflow.

67
00:10:25,100 --> 00:10:41,190
To conclude, this platform integrates natural-language product requirements, structured ingredient intelligence, Knowledge Graph-based retrieval, constraint-aware candidate selection, and formulation evaluation into a unified workflow.

68
00:10:41,140 --> 00:10:51,863
The major contribution is the integration of food knowledge and formulation constraints to support more systematic and explainable food product development.

69
00:10:51,863 --> 00:11:04,730
The platform is designed to assist food scientists and product developers in exploring ingredient combinations, evaluating formulation candidates, and making informed decisions.

70
00:11:04,730 --> 00:11:19,044
Future work will focus on improving retrieval accuracy, expanding the ingredient knowledge base, strengthening formulation evaluation models, and validating generated candidates through laboratory experiments.

71
00:11:19,044 --> 00:11:22,267
Thank you for watching this demonstration.

